. ** UPDATE: Hypothesis test with new generated dependent variable instead of t
> hree separated ones **
.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         /* We consider attitudes towards the following two statements as good
>  measures of support for gender equality: 1) Children below the age of 6 suff
> er if their mother works (plh0298_v2); 2) Children below the age of 3 suffer 
> if their mother works (plh0302_v2). We combine the two items into one variabl
> e by adding them together and reverse the direction the variable (ranging fro
> m 2 to 14): the higher the value, the stronger the support for gender equalit
> y. We thereby obtain our dependent variable (gndrequal)
>         
>         The original items (plh0298_v2 & plh0302_v2) are only availabe in 201
> 8 (They were also surveyed in 2012 but on a much smaller, completely differen
> t group of participants.). Therefore, we run linear OLS regresion analysis fo
> r the dependent variable (gndrequal), applying sampling weights (phrf) and st
> andard errors at the household level. Our independent variable kidsex_all has
>  three categories denoting whether having biological daughters in the survey 
> year: `No daughter', `Only daughter(s), and `Mixed'. 
>         
>         We also include the following control variables: gender, age group, e
> ducation, employment status, net household income, marital status, number of 
> biological children, whether youngest biological child <= 3 years, region (We
> st or East Germany), and migration background.
>         */
. 
.         reg gndrequal i.kidsex_all##ib2.sex i.age_gr i.educ_3cat i.emplst_4ca
> t i.hinc_5cat i.famstd_3cat i.sumkids_4cat i.youngest0to3_d i.sampreg i.migba
